#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130150} :activity/id #uuid "15f10ca2-71ff-5122-8f23-034d870b20ee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049796 :topic/id #uuid "1478960d-9b90-58d8-9424-023723660815" :topic/math-academy-id 2034} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130150 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130432 :tutorial/content "When adding numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the sum is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational. Rational numbers can be represented as fractions of the form $\\dfrac ab$ (where $a$ and $b$ are integers), and adding two of these fractions always produces another fraction.\n- The sum of a rational and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$\\sqrt 2 + \\dfrac12$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the sum in decimal form, we get\n$1.414213562… + 0.5 = 1.914213562…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following sum is irrational:\n$\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt 3 = 3.146\\,264\\,37\\ldots$\nHowever, the following sum is rational:\n$\\sqrt 2 + (-\\sqrt 2) = 0$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1b2b00f9-12fd-5755-9340-1a9477f61cdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5941 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "37af3d14-d0da-5833-8cc2-8d34a27c4558" :step/math-academy-id 36260 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130161}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129054 :question/id #uuid "d6839658-bf90-55e5-845c-c3ded22c7735" :question/math-academy-id "e-9672"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf0b9c3-84ba-51c8-bb7e-a45a441834ea" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050300}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518210}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Sum or Difference of Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "8ae24fd7-3348-5c2c-97a8-bf4a352b05a5" :step/math-academy-id 36261 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129236 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129055 :question/id #uuid "bed9f966-a72e-56d4-acb9-3e156d763c96" :question/math-academy-id "e-9674"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b04804-a1f3-57de-b966-65074210fc6b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233424}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Product or Quotient of Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "6e1aa31d-da89-5630-8461-cb2becfe2266" :step/math-academy-id 36263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130433 :tutorial/content "When multiplying real numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the product is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The product of two rational numbers is *always* rational since the result must be a fraction of the form $\\dfrac ab$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers.\n- The product of a *nonzero* rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$2\\cdot \\sqrt 2$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the product in decimal form, we get\n$2 \\cdot 1.414213562… = 2.828427125…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following product is irrational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{3}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 3}; = \\sqrt{6}$\nHowever, the following product is rational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 2}; = \\sqrt{2^{2}}; = 2$" :tutorial/id #uuid "74425e69-1e7a-5c1c-b1ac-2aec2e2ad1df" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5942 :tutorial/title "Products of Rational and Irrational Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "f1b0c4be-3561-5047-b474-fc789baf401e" :step/math-academy-id 36266 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130162}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130161 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130437 :tutorial/content "To summarize:\n\n- the sum (or difference) of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the sum (or difference) of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the sum (or difference) of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational." :tutorial/id #uuid "df9f71cf-81c8-546c-99f3-cca45192f745" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5998 :tutorial/title "A Summary"} :step/id #uuid "120df569-6a65-5f8d-9c7d-5fcfadd9b271" :step/math-academy-id 36625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130151}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130162 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130438 :tutorial/content "To summarize:\n\n- the product (or quotient) of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the product (or quotient) of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the product (or quotient) of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nFinally, it's worth pointing out that if $a$ is irrational, then the second rule implies that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1a\n$$\n\nis also irrational." :tutorial/id #uuid "45f6a1bf-6d91-5130-8aa5-a2d5863e2654" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5999 :tutorial/title "A Summary"} :step/id #uuid "d13633da-c1a6-540e-a940-eff75190b892" :step/math-academy-id 36626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130152}}] :activity/title "Sums and Products of Rational and Irrational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129373}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518234} :activity/id #uuid "a2a8669c-ee96-56c4-bb5a-a6fd2bdf735e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049796 :topic/id #uuid "1478960d-9b90-58d8-9424-023723660815" :topic/math-academy-id 2034} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518234 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8bfb4dd-e556-5d6f-aaef-fddc9a598988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126207} {:answer/id #uuid "bee69923-eae2-5ddf-a42b-d1b058dc8af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126208} {:answer/id #uuid "db97e4bf-99f0-504e-a725-2a9b37da3c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126209} {:answer/id #uuid "672fa625-0676-5877-87a0-a3ecd00928cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126210} {:answer/id #uuid "767cf28e-15b6-599c-8613-52500710d20b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186126207} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9b62cac-9385-5bff-8c35-d1b52ef42ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "18fad891-cd2e-5b5f-9e81-fba725a5c444" :question/math-academy-id "q-133430" :question/problem "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $17$ is rational\n\nII. $17a$ is rational\n\nIII. $a\\cdot 17$ is irrational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- the product of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. The product of a rational number ($17$) and an irrational number ($a$) is irrational.\n\n- Statement III is true. We know from part II that $17a$ is irrational. And since multiplication is commutative, we conclude that $a\\cdot 17$ is irrational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "2e9d24d0-afef-510e-9520-1823a6868cfd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518262}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518245 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b1658d7-fb9d-5945-a721-88c70ad2282a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517510} {:answer/id #uuid "6968ee8d-91bd-5ab9-bb4d-43e24c86d97a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517681} {:answer/id #uuid "8638d565-4f58-5ed6-9bb5-a0a33a0508a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517740} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0a4dea-a87b-5f8a-a8c1-8a1b2cb1d7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517839} {:answer/id #uuid "d3fa6e00-f1ae-53d5-ba58-bc05ad657bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517886}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517839} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b4cee67-86ab-5077-982d-73159c288590" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e5ec21fc-d95b-5a6e-8483-328c912290e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-133671" :question/problem "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is irrational\n\nII. $a-\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is always rational\n\nIII. $a+\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is sometimes rational and sometimes irrational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- the sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $-\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their sum or difference is always an irrational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "3d764519-dc05-5945-a5e0-05d704da56e5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518262 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4afb3715-3f88-56d5-b275-bc81df1d0871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517633} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc3bc0f-8609-527a-aef7-251c33edf7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517637} {:answer/id #uuid "592eee93-53ea-5ef8-a416-d9e903eec0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517654} {:answer/id #uuid "6f10f982-4e9f-520e-8314-481be6073a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517702} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8a4a04-79cb-50a8-82a9-14ab1f07c5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517637} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbb07e29-0dfc-5f19-8c86-10222960800a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2b569a4a-634a-5c5d-8e62-c4297125cda8" :question/math-academy-id "q-133670" :question/problem "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{7}$ is irrational\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{7}+a$ is always irrational\n\nIII. $a-\\frac{1}{7}$ is sometimes rational and sometimes irrational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- the sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\frac{1}{7}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their sum is always an irrational number.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\frac{1}{7}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their difference is always an irrational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "777d28dd-d1bc-5505-b0d0-f28dac65000a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518245}}] :activity/title "Sums and Products of Rational and Irrational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517490}]}
